# Part Three — The Examiner's Record of Topic 4

## Every question, 2016–2026

@py topic_questions 4

## What the record reveals

- **The heaviest topic: 16 full questions and 3 short notes** (==17.5 visits==), and at least one question **every year** from 2016 to 2026. It also supplies 17 of the 140 MCQs.
- **Four strong families.** **WID/WAD/GAD** (2016, 2017 note, 2018 note on needs, 2021, 2025); **colonial and capitalist perspectives** (2020, 2022, 2023, 2024 — four years running); **globalisation** (2017, 2018, 2019, 2025); **development theories** (2019, 2019 note, 2021, 2024, 2025).
- **Critique is the command.** "Critically review", "critically analyse", "critical gender analysis", "critically evaluate". Description alone cannot score well.
- **The questions join theory to the present.** Development as coloniality (2024); gendered globalisation with world-system theory (2025); climate change, floods and heat waves (2026).
- **Near repeats.** WID/WAD/GAD in 2016 and 2021; colonial and capitalist perspectives in 2023 and 2024.
- **Never asked in full:** world-system theory alone, gender mainstreaming, practical versus strategic needs as a full question, the SDGs, the care economy.

## Five likely questions

1. "Explain world-system theory. How does it explain the position of women in the global economy?" *(Chapter 6.)*
2. "Distinguish between practical gender needs and strategic gender interests. Why does the distinction matter for development planning in Pakistan?" *(Chapter 11.)*
3. "What is gender mainstreaming? Critically evaluate its achievements since Beijing 1995." *(Chapter 11.)*
4. "Assess the gendered impact of IMF-supported stabilisation programmes on women in Pakistan." *(Chapter 12.)*
5. "Unpaid care work is the hidden foundation of the economy. Discuss with reference to feminist economics." *(Chapters 3 and 12; Part Two.)*

These are not solved here; the Prediction Papers include answers to predicted questions. Plan each as an answer map for practice.
